# main is production

Every push to `main` deploys machiilabs.com. There is no staging step between `main` and the public.

## Never, without the user's explicit words for this exact action

- Push, merge, fast-forward, cherry-pick, rebase, or revert anything on `main`.
- "commit", "push", "ship the app", "release Skagway", or finishing a task is **not** permission to touch `main`. Only "merge <branch> to main", "push to main", or "ship the site" counts, and it covers that one action.
- Force-push or reset `main`, ever, unless the user asks for that by name.

If you believe something should go to `main`, stop and ask. Say which branch and exactly what it changes on the live site.

## Every new branch starts from origin/main

```bash
git fetch origin main
git switch -c <name> origin/main
```

- Never branch from whatever is checked out, and never from a release branch such as `skagway-1.3.0`. That is how the 1.3.0 site shipped early: a pond-guide branch was cut from `skagway-1.3.0`, then fast-forwarded onto `main`, carrying every unreleased 1.3.0 page with it.
- Before starting unrelated work, run `git status -sb` and check the current branch.
- Check a branch before proposing it for `main`: `git log --oneline origin/main..<branch>` must list only that task's commits.

## Release branches (`skagway-X.Y.Z`)

- Hold unreleased site and manual copy for Skagway X.Y.Z. They merge to `main` only when the user says to ship that release's site, after the notarized DMG exists and `skagway-download.ts` has the real build and SHA-256 (see `skagway-site-versioning.mdc`).
- Keep them current by merging `origin/main` into them, never the other way round.

## If unreleased work reaches main anyway

Stop and tell the user. Do not improvise fixes on `main`.

Reverting it on `main` leaves the release commits in `main`'s history, so a later merge of the release branch brings back nothing. After any revert, rebuild the release branch on top of the new `main` by re-applying its full diff, and verify that `git diff origin/main <release-branch>` is exactly the unreleased work.

## Guards

- `scripts/git-hooks/pre-push` blocks pushes to `main` unless `MACHII_MAIN_PUSH=yes`. It also requires `MACHII_SHIP_SKAGWAY=X.Y.Z` for pushes that carry commits from `skagway-X.Y.Z`, change the public download version, or turn the coming-soon promo on or off, and it rejects a non-numeric download build. Set these only after the user's explicit approval. Never use `--no-verify` to get past it.
- Install on every clone: `bash scripts/install-git-hooks.sh`.
- GitHub branch protection on `main` (pull request required, no force push) is the backstop that agents cannot bypass.
